/*******************************************************************************/
/
/ ABACUS constraint examples: fixed-mu dipole, charged ionic systems
/
/*******************************************************************************/

This directory demonstrates the real-space constraint framework with:

1. `01_fixed_mu_dipole`  -- a dipole-saw constraint with a fixed Lagrange
   multiplier, and the equivalent external electric field;
2. `02_charged_ionic`    -- a localized charged ionic system (`LiCl+`) with
   two per-atom MBIS charge constraints;
3. `03_charged_anion_cutoff` -- a single `Cl-` anion showing why the MBIS
   pro-density cutoff matters for diffuse charge tails.

The examples use `device cpu` in INPUT.  On a GPU build add `device gpu`
(and keep `precision double`); MBIS then runs on the GPU backend.  Note that
ABACUS forces `kpar = NPROC` on GPU; with a single Gamma k-point and
`ABACUS_NPROCS > 1` this leaves some ranks without a k-point.  For the GPU
check use `ABACUS_NPROCS=1` (or a multi-k KPT).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Fixed mu can stand in for an electric field
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

`01_fixed_mu_dipole/INPUT` uses a dipole-saw constraint with

    "fixed_mu": -0.01        (Ry / e*Bohr, in constraint_target.json)

A fixed-mu run skips the outer multiplier loop: the constraint potential is
the ordinary external potential `mu * w(r)`.  For the saw frame
`w(r) = -Lz * saw(z)`, which is exactly a saw electric field.

`INPUT.efield` is the same physical system with the ABACUS electric-field
feature.  For the cubic cell used here (`LATTICE_VECTORS = diag(15)`), the
two potentials coincide when

    efield_amp = -mu / 2        (Hartree)

so `mu = -0.01` corresponds to `efield_amp = +0.005`, with
`efield_pos_max = 0.9` and `efield_pos_dec = 0.1` matching
`constraint_dipole_saw_pos_max/dec`.

To compare:

    cd 01_fixed_mu_dipole
    bash run.sh                     # fixed-mu constraint
    cp INPUT INPUT.constraint.keep
    cp INPUT.efield INPUT
    bash run.sh                     # external field
    mv INPUT.constraint.keep INPUT  # restore the constraint input

Expected (double precision):

    fixed mu : FINAL_ETOT ~ -642.7326307 eV
    efield   : FINAL_ETOT ~ -642.7326307 eV
    difference ~ 1e-8 eV (parallel details may move the last digits)

The total energies agree to numerical precision; the forces agree to
0.002-0.005 eV/Angstrom (the two force implementations differ slightly).
A fixed multiplier is therefore a valid field stand-in.  The mapping
`efield_amp = -mu/2` is specific to a cubic cell with `e33 = 1`; for a
general cell use `efield_amp = -mu * Lz * bmod / (e2 * lat0)` with
`e2 = 2`, `Lz = latvec.e33 * lat0` and `bmod` from `Efield::prepare`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Charged, localized ionic system: LiCl+
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

`02_charged_ionic` uses `nelec_delta = -1.0`, i.e. a total of 9 electrons
(LiCl+).  The hole is localized on the ionic molecule, and two MBIS charge
constraints transfer 0.05 e from Cl to Li:

    Li: target +0.05,   Cl: target -0.05   (delta mode)

Run:

    cd 02_charged_ionic
    bash run.sh

Expected audit (final converged frame):

    total_charge ~ 8.9999        nelec = 9
    residual     ~ 6e-4 e        (CONVERGED, constraint_thr = 1e-3)
    # The exact residual depends on the SCF budget/parallel split; if the run
    # stops at "budget freeze", raise scf_nmax or constraint_step_max.

The residual decreases monotonically every outer step.  A localized ionic
charge (or a charged defect) is a good use case for this constraint.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Diffuse charge: why the MBIS cutoff matters
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

`03_charged_anion_cutoff` is a single Cl- anion (`nelec = 7`,
`nelec_delta = +1`).  The extra electron is bound but has a diffuse tail.
The input uses the default `constraint_mbis_cutoff = 10.0` Bohr:

    cd 03_charged_anion_cutoff
    bash run.sh                     # cutoff = 10
    bash run_cut20.sh               # cutoff = 20

Expected:

    cutoff 10: total_charge ~ 7.998423, deficit ~ 1.58e-3 e
               (warning.log: "MBIS per-atom charges sum to ... but nelec ...")
    cutoff 20: total_charge ~ 7.999999819, deficit ~ 1.8e-7 e, no warning

This is the practical diagnostic for charged/diffuse systems: the constraint
enforces the MBIS partition, but density outside the pro cutoff is not
assigned to any atom.  Increase `constraint_mbis_cutoff` until
`sum(Q) == nelec` (the code warns once at a converged SCF frame), or use a
scheme with an explicit remainder channel.

Guidance:
  * localized ionic charge / defects  -> constraint is appropriate;
  * strongly diffuse states (extra electron delocalized over a large box,
    near-continuum states) -> a local charge constraint is not the right
    tool; enlarge the cutoff/remainder or choose another scheme.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Running all examples
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Edit `../SETENV` first (`ABACUS_PATH`, `ABACUS_NPROCS`, `ABACUS_THREADS`),
then:

    bash runall.sh

Note: the MBIS examples can be expensive on a CPU.  For a quick check,
reduce `ecutwfc/ecutrho` and/or `scf_nmax`, or run on a GPU build with
`device gpu`.
